  "summary": "Africa's earliest sculpture tradition: serene life-sized terracotta heads with triangular eyes.",
  "description": "Nok terracottas — naturalistic heads and figures with piercing triangular eyes, elaborately coiffed hair, beads and basketry hats — appear from around 1500 BCE to 500 CE on the Jos plateau, West Africa's earliest known sculptural tradition and contemporaneous with early iron smelting. Farmers uncovered them in tin-mining gravel; archaeologists (Bernard Fagg above all) pieced the culture together through thermoluminescence dating. Fragmentary, weathered, and haunting, Nok heads anchor Nigerian museums and every survey of African art history.",
